    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2026.1782742",
    "abstract": "This study demonstrates that elevation gradients modulate the temporal stability of climate-growth relationships and underscores that the responses of alpine forest ecosystems to climate change are dynamic rather than static. These findings provide a novel framework for understanding the dynamic adaptive mechanisms of montane ecosystems in the Qilian Mountains under ongoing climate change and offer critical insights for designing adaptive forest management strategies in high-elevation cold regions.",
    "field": "Dendroclimatology",
    "problem": "The mechanisms governing the unstable and elevation-dependent growth responses of *Picea crassifolia* to climate variability in the Qilian Mountains are incompletely understood, raising concerns about the resilience of high-elevation forests in this ecologically sensitive region.",
    "method": "This study developed tree-ring width chronologies across five elevation bands and applied 30-year moving window correlation analyses with gridded climate data to systematically evaluate the temporal stability of climate-growth relationships along an elevational gradient.",
    "task": "The task is to evaluate the temporal stability of climate-growth relationships for *Picea crassifolia* along an elevation gradient in the Qilian Mountains using tree-ring chronologies and gridded climate data.",
    "model": [
        "CRU TS"
    ],
